The results of a cross-country team's training run are graphed below. Which student has the greatest average speed? [asy]
for ( int i = 1; i <= 7; ++i )
{

draw((i,0)--(i,6));
}

for ( int i = 1; i <= 5; ++i )
{

draw((0,i)--(8,i));
}
draw((-0.5,0)--(8,0), linewidth(1));
draw((0,-0.5)--(0,6), linewidth(1));
label("$O$", (0,0), SW);
label(scale(.85)*rotate(90)*"distance", (0, 3), W);
label(scale(.85)*"time", (4, 0), S);
dot((1.25, 4.5));
label(scale(.85)*"Evelyn", (1.25, 4.8), N);
dot((2.5, 2.2));
label(scale(.85)*"Briana", (2.5, 2.2), S);
dot((4.25,5.2));
label(scale(.85)*"Carla", (4.25, 5.2), SE);
dot((5.6, 2.8));
label(scale(.85)*"Debra", (5.6, 2.8), N);
dot((6.8, 1.4));
label(scale(.85)*"Angela", (6.8, 1.4), E);
[/asy]

positive:
Average speed is computed as
\[
\text{average speed} = \frac{\text{distance}}{\text{time}},
\]
which is the slope of the line from the origin \(O\) to each student's point on the distance–time graph.

From the Asymptote code, the coordinates are:
- Evelyn: \((1.25,\;4.5)\)
- Briana: \((2.5,\;2.2)\)
- Carla: \((4.25,\;5.2)\)
- Debra: \((5.6,\;2.8)\)
- Angela: \((6.8,\;1.4)\)

Compute distance/time for each:

- Evelyn:
\[
\frac{4.5}{1.25}
= \frac{\frac{9}{2}}{\frac{5}{4}}
= \frac{9}{2}\cdot\frac{4}{5}
= \frac{36}{10}
= 3.6
\]

- Briana:
\[
\frac{2.2}{2.5}
= \frac{\frac{11}{5}}{\frac{5}{2}}
= \frac{11}{5}\cdot\frac{2}{5}
= \frac{22}{25}
= 0.88
\]

- Carla:
\[
\frac{5.2}{4.25}
= \frac{\frac{26}{5}}{\frac{17}{4}}
= \frac{26}{5}\cdot \frac{4}{17}
= \frac{104}{85}
\approx 1.224
\]

- Debra:
\[
\frac{2.8}{5.6}
= \frac{\frac{14}{5}}{\frac{28}{5}}
= \frac{14}{5}\cdot\frac{5}{28}
= \frac{14}{28}
= 0.5
\]

- Angela:
\[
\frac{1.4}{6.8}
= \frac{\frac{7}{5}}{\frac{34}{5}}
= \frac{7}{5}\cdot\frac{5}{34}
= \frac{7}{34}
\approx 0.206
\]

Comparing these speeds, Evelyn’s value \(3.6\) is the greatest.

\[
\boxed{\text{Evelyn}}
\]

